          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            What is called
            %em KR-theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#Atiyah66"}> Atiyah 66
            ) is variant of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces"} spaces
            equipped with a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            (by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/involution"} involutive
            homeomorphism – a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+space"}> real space
            ”).
          %p
            In terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
            models, classes of KR-theory are represented by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+bundles"} complex vector bundles
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            on which the involution on their base space lifts to an
            %em anti-linear
            involution of the total space. Over manifolds with trivial involution these are precisely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complexification"} complexification
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+vector+bundles"} real vector bundles
            and hence over such spaces
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi KR
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KR
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KO"} KO
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∪
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \cup X
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            equipped with the involution that interchanges the two,
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi KR
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KR
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KU"} KU
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times S^1
            with the involution the antipodal identification on the second (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"}> circle
            ) factor ,
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi KR
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KR
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KSC-theory"} KSC-theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#Anderson64"}> Anderson 64
            ). So in general
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi KR
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KR
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times S^1
            with the reflection-involution on the circle (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+space"} real space
            denoted
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{1,1}
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+sphere"} representation sphere
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi KO
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KO
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/O-planes"} O-planes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi KU
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KU
            in their complement (a model that makes this very explicit is given in
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#DMR13"} DMR 13, section 4
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi KR
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi KO
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo −
                  %mi KU
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo −
                  %mi KO
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  KR(S^{1,1}) = ( KO --- KU --- KO )
          %p
            More abstractly, complex conjugation of complex vector bundles induces on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+K-theory"} complex K-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KU"} KU
            an involutive
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism"} automorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi KR
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KR
            is the corresponding
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+spectrum"} equivariant spectrum
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi KR
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KR
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant cohomology
            theory. In particular, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fixed+point"} homotopy fixed point
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KU"} KU
            under this automorphism is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KO"} KO
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi KO
                  %mo ≃
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi KU
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  KO \simeq (KU)^{\mathbb{Z}/2}
          %p
            (e.g.
            %a{:href => "#Karoubi01"}> Karoubi 01
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Dugger03"} Dugger 03, corollary 7.6
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#HillHopkinsRavenel"} Hill-Hopkins-Ravenel, section 7.3
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KU"} KU
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-modules"}> ∞-modules
            ), so in KR-theory one has
            = succeed "-modules." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi KO
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KO
          %p
            KR is an example of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real-oriented+cohomology+theory"} real-oriented cohomology theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MR-theory"} MR-theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BPR-theory"} BPR-theory
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_on_terminology Remark on terminology
            %p
              An
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/involution"} involution
              on a space by a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphism
              (or
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
              = succeed "\u201D," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+structure"} real structure
              = succeed "\u201D." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+spaces"} real spaces
              = succeed "-theory" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi KR
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KR
              %strong beware
              that this terminology easily conflicts with or is confused with
              = succeed "-theory." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KO"} KO
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#as_the_grothendieck_group_of_complex_vector_bundles_with_real_structure As the Grothendieck group of complex vector bundles with real structure
          %p
            …(
            %a{:href => "#Atiyah66"}> Atiyah 66
            )…
          %h3#as_a_genuine_spectrum
            As a genuine
            = succeed "-Spectrum" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
          %p
            The following gives
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi KR
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KR
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genuine+G-spectrum"} genuine G-spectrum
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G = \mathbb{Z}_2
          %p
            Using that every orthogonal representation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
            is contained in an
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^n
            with its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+conjugation"} complex conjugation
            action, one can restrict attention to these. Write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi ℂ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{C}P^1 = S^{2,1} = S^{\mathbb{C}}
                  \\,.
          %p
            The reduced
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+line+bundle"} canonical line bundle
            over this (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+fibration"} Hopf fibration
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo ×
                  %mi BU
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S^{2,1}= \mathbb{C}P^1 \to \mathbb{Z}\times BU
          %p
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            = succeed "-spectrum" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo ×
                  %mi BU
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo ×
                  %mi BU
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S^{2,1} \wedge (\mathbb{Z} \times BU)\to \mathbb{Z}\times BU
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is in fact an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Omega+spectrum"} Omega spectrum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bott+periodicity"} Bott periodicity
            (for instance in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Dugger03"} Dugger 03, p. 2-3
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#bigrading Bigrading
          %p
            As any genuine
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology+theory"} equivariant cohomology theory
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi KR
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KR
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+ring"} representation ring
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi RO
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RO(\mathbb{Z}_2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            for the trivial 1-dimensional representation and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}_-
            for that given by the sign involution. Then the general orthogonalrepresentation decomposes as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo +
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo −
                    %mi q
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  V = \mathbb{R}^+\oplus \mathbb{R}_-^q
                  \\,.
          %p
            The corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+sphere"} representation sphere
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi V
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi some
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi convention
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S^V = (some\; convention)
                  \\,.
          %h3#InducedFromModuliStackOfTori As induced from the derived moduli stack of tori
          %p
            The relation between
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi KU
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KU
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi KO
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KO
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi KR
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KR
            naturally arises in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chromatic+homotopy+theory"} chromatic homotopy theory
            as follows.
          %p
            Inside the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack+of+formal+group+laws"} moduli stack of formal group laws
            sits the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack+of+tori"} moduli stack of one dimensional tori
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝔾
                        %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}_{\mathbb{G}_m}
            (
            %a{:href => "#LawsonNaumann12"}> Lawson-Naumann 12, def. A.1, A.3
            ). This is equivalent to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+stack"} quotient stack
            of the point by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+of+order+2"} group of order 2
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝔾
                        %mi m
                  %mo ≃
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{M}_{\mathbb{G}_m}\simeq \mathbf{B}\mathbb{Z}_2
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#LawsonNaumann12"}> Lawson-Naumann 12, prop. A.4
            ). Here the
            = succeed "-action" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
          %p
            Using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Goerss-Hopkins-Miller+theorem"} Goerss-Hopkins-Miller theorem
            this stack carries an
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+ring"} E-∞ ring
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+sheaf"} structure sheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}^{top}
            (
            %a{:href => "#LawsonNaumann12"}> Lawson-Naumann 12, theorem A.5
            ); and by the above equivalence this is a single
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+ring"} E-∞ ring
            equipped with a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-action"} ∞-action
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KU"} KU
            with its involution induced by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+conjugation"} complex conjugation
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi KR
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KR
          %p
            Accordingly, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+sections"} global sections
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}^{top}
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝔾
                        %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}_{\mathbb{G}_m}
            are the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fixed+points"} homotopy fixed points
            of this action, hence is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi KO
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KO
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Mathew13"} Mathew 13, section 3
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Mathew"} Mathew, section 2
          %p
            As suggested there and by the main (
            %a{:href => "#LawsonNaumann12"}> Lawson-Naumann 12, theorem 1.2
            ) this realizes (at least localized at
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p = 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi KO
                  %mo →
                  %mi KU
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KO \to KU
            as the restriction of an analogous inclusion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tmf"} tmf
            built as the global sections of the similarly derived
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack+of+elliptic+curves"} moduli stack of elliptic curves
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real-oriented+cohomology+theory"} real-oriented cohomology theory
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MR-theory"} MR-theory
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BPR-theory"} BPR-theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+algebraic+K-theory"} real algebraic K-theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientifold"} orientifold
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KO-dimension"} KO-dimension
          %div
            %p
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chromatic+homotopy+theory"} chromatic homotopy theory
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chromatic+level"} chromatic level
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+oriented+cohomology+theory"} complex oriented cohomology theory
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+ring"}> E-∞ ring
                    \/
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-%E2%88%9E+ring"} A-∞ ring
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+oriented+cohomology+theory"} real oriented cohomology theory
              %tbody
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 0
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+spectrum"} Eilenberg-MacLane spectrum
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi H
                          %mi ℤ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \mathbb{Z}
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HZR-theory"} HZR-theory
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    0th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morava+K-theory"} Morava K-theory
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi K
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 0
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(0)
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+K-theory"} complex K-theory
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+K-theory+spectrum"} complex K-theory spectrum
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi KU
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} KU
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KR-theory"} KR-theory
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    first
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morava+K-theory"} Morava K-theory
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi K
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(1)
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
